# Autocal optimization algorithm

`autocal.py` runs the full-auto loop.
`planning_pass.py` fits the current dataset and ranks possible next sweeps.
Spool fitting is optional. Both `--find-radii` and `--find-buildup-factor`
default to `off`.

## Loop names

| Name | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Dataset-growth iteration | Fit the current dataset, select a run, then accept or add a sweep. |
| Run variant | One solver/settings combination on the current dataset. |
| Planning pass | One recalibration plus next-sweep ranking. |
| Filter pass | One spool/anchor fit under a chosen filtering regime. |
| Alternating refinement round | One spool step plus an optional anchor proposal. |
| Spool coordinate-descent round | One pass over the fitted spool parameters. |

With spool fitting enabled, the nesting is:

```text
dataset-growth iteration
  run variant
    planning pass
      filter pass
        alternating refinement round
          spool coordinate-descent rounds
```

Without spool fitting, a planning pass calls `calibrate_elliptical(...)` directly.
It skips the spool, filter-schedule, and objective-schedule machinery.

## Full-auto loop

If the dataset is missing, the collector bootstraps up to three representative
sweeps. It tunes force and travel unless collector settings override tuning.
If an existing dataset has more than three sweeps, replay starts with its first
three. Replay adds one stored sweep per iteration in a `.replay_tmp.json` file.
The original dataset stays untouched during replay.

Each iteration runs the requested variants on the current dataset.
`--full-auto-run` supplies variants. `--shotgun` appends entries from `shotgun.conf`.
Without variants, one run uses the CLI settings.
Each planning pass recalibrates from the current data.
Ordinary iterations do not warm-start from the previous iteration's solution.
Filter passes and recovery attempts do use warm starts.

Run validity requires finite cost, rank, and covariance diagnostics.
Selection prefers constrained runs, then lower `rank_score`.
Relative uncertainty, primary cost, and run ID break ties.
Only the selected constrained plan from each iteration enters history.

Patience uses `history_rank_score`.
It resets when the current plan ranks first in history.
The default patience is three non-improving iterations.
Optional cost or uncertainty thresholds can also request acceptance.
An underconstrained selection continues toward more data after recovery attempts.

If continuing, replay adds one stored sweep.
After replay is exhausted, the loop switches to live collection.
Live collection uses the selected plan's suggested command and merges its output.
`--no-collect` stops before live collection. It requires an existing dataset to
avoid the initial bootstrap.

Acceptance runs final history selection, prints the summary, and sends M669 to RRF.
The summary prints fitted M666 parameters but does not send them.
Klipper skips M669. `--sim --no-collect` also skips sending unless a server was
explicitly supplied.
Ctrl-C during the fitting/collection loop requests best-so-far acceptance.
A `<dataset-stem>.full_auto.stop` file requests the same action.
Reaching `--max-steps` only ends the loop. It does not run final history selection
or send a calibration. The default limit is 20 iterations.

## Prediction at final acceptance

Commit `4daac40c` added prediction-based history selection.
Commit `517523e5` added model-specific validation and a shared anchor comparison.
These checks run at acceptance. They do not run before each new sweep.
They do not change within-iteration selection, next-sweep ranking, or patience.

For each saved candidate, validation excludes all of its training sweep IDs.
It scores the remaining whole sweeps from the latest saved history plan.
Anchors and spool parameters stay frozen.
Filtering stays off, and a robust loss keeps every valid observation in the score.
Invalid geometry, missing residuals, or nonfinite results fail validation.

The own-model score uses raw data, the candidate's spool transform, and its
measurement-model settings. It is logged as `heldout_prediction`.
The shared comparison uses the latest plan's transformed estimation dataset
and the validation helper's default settings. It is logged as `anchor_comparison`.

Candidates with a finite own-model prediction take priority over candidates
with failed or unavailable predictions.
If predictions were attempted but all failed, no calibration is applied.
If none has held-out data, selection uses the history score.

Final ordering compares `anchor_comparison`, `heldout_prediction`,
`history_rank_score`, `rank_score`, relative uncertainty, primary cost,
and then prefers a later iteration. Each candidate can have a different
held-out sweep set. Unreplayed sweeps are not used for this check.

See `objective_functions_overview` for score definitions and display mappings.

## Planning pass

`plan_next_ellipse_sweep(...)` loads the dataset and normalizes drive/sensor roles.
It builds an anchor seed from machine settings, or accepts an explicit warm start.
It also annotates the noise model.

If spool fitting is enabled, it requires raw encoder angles.
It first solves seed anchors on the base dataset.
Then it runs the spool/filter pipeline on modeled lengths.
It uses the last filter pass's final calibration when available.
Otherwise, it runs a final anchor calibration on the transformed dataset.

The pass estimates an anchor information matrix and covariance.
It generates candidate sweeps, removes duplicates and candidates too close to
existing fixed deltas, and ranks the remaining candidates by D-optimal gain.
This gain is the increase in the information matrix's log determinant.
Higher gain is better. It is distinct from held-out prediction error.

With spool fitting, information is evaluated in modeled lengths.
The suggested collection command still uses the collector's base-length coordinates.
Force settings from the dataset are reused. The command requests return to origin.

## Filter and objective schedules

The default schedules are:

```text
--filter-schedule warmup,warmup,warmup,dynamic
--objective-schedule 1,1,1,1
```

For schedules with multiple filter entries, `filter_pass.py` applies these rules:

| Pass | Runtime filtering | Dataset and mask |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `warmup` / `0` | Off | Use the full pass input. Clear any locked mask. |
| `dynamic` / `1` | Follow global pointwise/sweep flags | Use the full pass input. Rebuild the locked mask after fitting. |
| `constant` / `2` | Off | Reuse the mask from the latest successful dynamic pass. |

A constant pass needs a dynamic pass since the latest warmup.
`warmup,constant` fails parsing.
A valid schedule can still fail if the dynamic pass cannot build a mask.
A single-entry schedule bypasses this wrapper and uses the global filter flags.
The low-level helper also takes this direct path when no schedule is supplied.

Each wrapped pass runs a spool/anchor pipeline, then a pointwise anchor calibration.
The fitted anchors, radii, and buildup seed the next pass.
Only the first pass enables spool seed prefit and bootstrap anchor refresh.
The last pass supplies the final result. Pass scores are logged for diagnostics.

`--objective-schedule` selects the spool objective and anchor proposal objective
for each pass. `0` is ellipse prefit/comparison, `1` is pointwise forward modeling,
and `2` is position-reconstruction spread.
Words `prefit`, `pointwise`, and `position` are also accepted.
A single objective repeats across passes. Otherwise, supply one per filter pass.
A list containing only one repeated value is resized to the filter schedule.
The final pointwise anchor calibration does not use this schedule.

## Single-pass spool/anchor pipeline

`initialize_pass.py` initializes radii, buildup, and anchors from seeds or defaults.
An optional spool seed prefit minimizes an ellipse-fit proxy plus spool priors.
It uses a guarded coordinate search and keeps anchors fixed.
The optional bootstrap anchor refresh then proposes a new anchor layout.
It tests move fractions `1.0`, `0.5`, and `0.25`.
It accepts only an improvement in `_data_cost(...)`.

`alternating_refinement.py` runs `--spool-outer-iters` rounds (default three):

1. Evaluate the current model's data cost, scheduled objective, priors, and rank.
2. Hold anchors fixed. Search spool parameters for a lower scheduled objective
   plus priors. Try repair seeds if the starting score is invalid or a sentinel.
3. Attempt an anchor proposal only if this spool objective improved enough.
4. If `--scale-fix 1` is enabled, test a matching anchor rescale as the proposal
   seed. Use it only when it lowers the data cost.
5. Generate the scheduled anchor proposal with spool parameters fixed.
6. Test full and damped moves. Compare spool rank first, then data cost plus priors.
7. Compare the resulting model with the round start. Keep it or roll back the round.

The pipeline tracks its best model with the same rank comparator.
The scheduled objective can improve while the resulting round is rejected.

The spool coordinate search uses bounded line searches.
It samples the current value, interval endpoints, and midpoints.
It can refine an improving midpoint with a scalar search.
Successful large moves can grow the next search interval.
An unsuccessful coordinate halves its step.
It stops at step tolerance, a small-progress plateau, or `--spool-inner-iters`
(default 30 rounds).

## Uniform scale polish

`scale_polish.py` can search one shared scale for radii and anchors.
It keeps buildup fixed and respects radius bounds.
The search minimizes data cost plus priors.
Acceptance also requires the spool rank comparator to prefer the result.
It needs radius fitting to be enabled.

`--scale-fix 3` runs the polish after refinement in every filter pass.
This is the default. Level `2` runs it only on the last pass.
Level `1` controls the anchor reseed inside refinement.
There is no scale polish inside each alternating round.

## Underconstrained recovery

The pointwise solver returns a finite sentinel when too few constraints remain.
Full-auto first retries with sweep filtering disabled.
It then retries with point filtering disabled, if needed.
Attempts warm-start from the preceding estimate.

`--sparse-recovery` adds a sparse seed fit.
It keeps endpoints and about half the points per drive direction, with at least
five when available. A valid sparse geometry seeds a fit on the full current data.
The sparse result itself is never the final recovery candidate.
Recovery rejects nonfinite, boundary, duplicate-anchor, and degenerate layouts.
If recovery fails, the loop continues toward more data.

## Pointwise solver and diagnostics

`calibrate.py` calls `ellipse_solver.py`.
With point filtering enabled, the solver uses wide-Huber, tight-Huber, and trim stages.
The first stage uses the restart budget. Later stages use the previous result.
Warmup passes disable this staged point filtering.
By default, each restart freezes sweep rejection at its starting guess.
The trim stage also freezes point rejection there.
`AUTOCAL_FREEZE_FILTER_MASKS=0` disables this local mask freezing.
This is separate from the mask shared across `dynamic` and `constant` filter passes.

`--solve-optimizer` selects `lbfgsb` (default), `lm`, or `trf`.
`--optimizer-mode fast` uses CPU JAX derivatives when available.
`fast-fd` uses the JAX value with finite differences.
`legacy` disables the JAX objective.

`line_model_fit` reports alternating and coordinate budgets.
`line_model_filter_schedule` reports pass order.
Selected-run logs distinguish `raw_fit_score_ui`, `rank_score`,
`history_rank_score`, and displayed `fit_score_ui`.
`history_select` records the final prediction-based choice.
The displayed quality score still comes from fit/history scoring.
It does not measure anchor accuracy.
